WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
Good Morning, John. This is one of those videos that you write fifteen times and you still aren't happy with,

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:10.160
but here goes. After Christina Grimmie was murdered, I... ran away from home. I went camping in a place where

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:15.060
there was no cell service. I spent that time with my friends, not wanting to confront the reality that someone

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:20.520
who shared my passions and ambitions had been killed doing what she loved, and something that I had just

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:28.280
spent the last two weeks of my life doing, meeting fans after a show. And I spent that time crying and feeling the

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:34.440
frailness of my own body in a way I never had before. Understanding how easy it is to have it all taken away.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:40.400
That, I imagine, is similar to how every police officer in America felt after the shootings in Dallas. Looking at

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.600
thier family and knowing that they could be taken away not just in the course of doing their job, but because of

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:51.740
what their job is. A difficult job that is usually done well. And now, the ones who have options of exploring other

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:57.720
careers, maybe they're thinking this job isn't so worth it anymore. And then I think of my neighbours and friends

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:03.460
and fellow creators who have felt the frailness of their own bodies for their whole lives, and the frailness of their

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:09.540
children's and their parents' bodies, not because of what they do, but because of the colour of their skin, because

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:15.140
of who they are. People who have lived their whole lives under that weight, because they're more likely to be seen

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:21.180
as a threat, because they know that any insignificant little move that could be seen as a suspicious action

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:26.360
could mean the end of them. I can never fully understand that experience. But I can listen, and I can be

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:30.880
open, and I can do some research myself. Research that shows that a black person being arrested by a police

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:36.520
officer is more likely to be shot, injured, pepper sprayed, tazed, and beaten. Research that shows that they're more likely

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:41.620
to be convicted of crimes and get plea deals that involve prison sentences. And that shows that once convicted,

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:46.900
they're more likely to receive longer, harsher sentences than the average American. We're promised that the law

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:52.600
sees all of us equally. But we know that's not true. We aren't that surprised when it's a young white man who

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:59.540
stands convicted of rape, that we hear from a judge about the loss of his future, and the terrible tragedy of all

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:03.840
of the opportunity that a harsh sentence would bring this person. But when it's a young black man convicted of

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.880
selling pot going to jail, well, that's not that big of a deal. We know this. We've seen this. It's in the experience of

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:14.060
every black American I've talked to about this, and the data consistently shows it. So when we say black lives

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:20.120
matter, we aren't just talking about Alton Sterling and Philando Castile and Michael Brown and Rekia Boyd

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:25.920
and Sandra Bland and Tamir Rice. We're talking about a fundamental and deep-seated and destructive belief in

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:32.160
this country, that black lives matter less. A belief that's expressed at every level of society, but as a white

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:38.540
person, I could go my entire life completely ignorant of. But that black Americans are fully aware of, and made

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:42.200
aware of, even as children. To the black members of this community, thank you for being a part of what we're

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.360
doing here. And thank you for your strength and your courage, and for sharing your experiences with us.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:51.580
A while back, John, you created a playlist of a bunch of different creators sharing their perspectives and

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:56.620
experiences on this stuff, and I've added to that and I've put it down in the description. We only get to have our

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:01.880
one life through which to experience the world, and we're lucky to be able to have this platform through which we

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:06.560
can understand other people's experiences of the world. So everybody, if you can, if you have time today, just take

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:13.720
some time to listen. Not to shout or judge or fight or dismiss, or even comment, just, that most

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:20.520
underappreciated verb in all of internet discourse, listen. It's not so bad. John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

